+Every release of `jobq` is written down here, newest first. The format follows
+[Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/), the version numbers follow
+[Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/), and one rule is enforced by the release tool rather
+than by review: a version whose section is missing, empty, or still sitting under `Unreleased` is a
+version the tool refuses to publish, because a number without an account of itself is a rollback
+nobody can reason about.
 
 ## [Unreleased]
 
-## [0.1.0] - TODO
+## [0.1.0] - 2026-10-05
 
-<!-- TODO: what this version contains. -->
+### Added
+
+- the engine as a library of its own: submitting, claiming, completing and failing jobs, with a
+  public surface that is what `src/engine/mod.rs` re-exports and nothing else.
+- two binaries around it: `jobqd`, the worker daemon that drains queues, and `jobqc`, the client an
+  operator or a script runs, with one exit code per failure class.
+- the store port with two implementations — an in-memory engine and a Postgres backend — held to
+  one conformance suite, so "the same question gets the same answer" is a test rather than a hope.
+- leases and recovery: a worker that dies mid-job leaves a lease to expire rather than a row stuck
+  in `running`, and a graceful shutdown drains what is in flight.
+- scheduling: priorities that do not starve a queue, cron schedules, delayed timers, and a dedup
+  window that collapses a duplicate submission.
+- queues with tenants and declarations: a handle only lists what it may see, and a refusal does not
+  tell a stranger which queues exist.
+- the dead-letter queue as an operator's view: `dead_letters()`, `replay`, and the audit trail a
+  replay leaves behind.
+- observability: an error budget with alerts that name their metric and window, structured logs that
+  a query can read, and traces that reach the worker that ran the job.
+- the project's own quality budgets: property tests over the state machine, a mutation threshold, a
+  coverage policy, a performance budget counted in work rather than seconds, and an unsafe budget of
+  zero.
+- the release itself: `jobq-release`, a `SHA256SUMS` per release, and this file.
