+Step 2 prints exactly the lines it wrote into `SHA256SUMS`, which is what a pipeline log should
+show: what was published, and what each file hashes to.
+
+## What the tool refuses
+
+[`jobq-release`](../src/bin/jobq-release.rs) builds nothing. It decides whether what it was handed
+*is* the release the tag names, and it refuses in three places:
+
+| the check | the failure it catches | exit |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| the tag is `v` and this crate's version | a release started from the wrong ref, or a tree whose version was never bumped | `2` |
+| each binary is there and reports the version when asked `--version` | a release assembled out of a stale build, or out of a directory the build never wrote | `3` / `1` |
+| `CHANGELOG.md` has a non-empty section for the version, with nothing left under `Unreleased` | a version number published without an account of itself | `1` |
+
+The third one is the check people are tempted to skip, and the reason it is here is that a tag can
+be moved but a changelog entry cannot be reconstructed from it afterwards: `Unreleased` is where a
+change is written down as it is made, and promoting it is what makes a release say what went out.
+
 ## What a user does with it
 
-<!-- TODO: how somebody who downloaded the release checks that the bytes are the ones we built,
-with a tool that is already on their machine, and what `jobqd` and `jobqc` are for. -->
+Download the two files, and check them before trusting either:
+
+```sh
+sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS
+export PATH="$PWD:$PATH"
+jobqd --check-config --config jobqd.toml   # refuses a file it cannot read, before it drains anything
+jobqc --help
+```
+
+`jobqd --version` and `jobqc --version` print the version this release is, which is the number to
+quote in a bug report — and the number the tool checked against the tag when the release was made.
+A release that verified is a release whose bytes are the ones that were built; a release that did
+not is a download to throw away, not to debug.
 
 ## Where this runs in a pipeline
 
-<!-- TODO: the job at the end of the pipeline from `mailer/1.3-ci`: what it is handed, what
-happens when the tool refuses, and why the release is started by a tag rather than by a branch. -->
+The job is the last step of the pipeline of `mailer/1.3-ci`, and it is started by a **tag** rather
+than by a branch: pushing `v0.1.0` starts a build, and the same tag is what the job hands the tool.
+If the tool refuses, the job fails and nothing is uploaded — which is the point. The exit codes are
+the table from [`jobq/3.15-cli-contract`](../src/cli/mod.rs): a `2` means the tag and the tree
+disagree and a human has to bump something, `3` means the build did not produce what the job
+promised, and `1` means the release itself did not check out.
 
 ## What a release does not carry yet
 
-<!-- TODO: signing, installers, and packaging per platform. Say what this project does instead of
-each of them, and why the checksum file is the part that had to be here. -->
+No signature and no per-platform packaging. A signature needs a key to manage, rotate and revoke,
+and it answers a question this project cannot answer yet — *who* built this — while the checksum
+file answers the one that matters first: whether the bytes arrived intact. Installers, archives and
+package-manager entries are what a real release adds on top of this shape
+([`cargo-dist`](https://opensource.axo.dev/cargo-dist/) is the tool that does it); the shape itself
+— two files, one manifest, one account of what changed — is what has to exist before any of them is
+worth wiring up.
+
+## How this is checked
+
+The release rehearsal is [`tests/release_harness.rs`](../tests/release_harness.rs) — a file this
+project's course ships, because a learner changing the instrument they are measured with is a
+learner changing the measurement. It runs the tool over the binaries this tree just built and
+prints four counts:
+
+```sh
+cargo test -p jobq --test release_harness -- --nocapture
+```
+
+| metric | what it counts | baseline |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `artifacts` | the binaries the release carries | 2 |
+| `checksum_lines` | the lines of the checksum file: one per binary | 2 |
+| `unverified_max` | binaries the checksum file does not cover: an upper bound | 0 |
+| `extras_max` | files the release carries that are not one of the two binaries: an upper bound | 0 |
+
+`kiln check jobq/3.25-release-artifacts --slow` runs that command and compares each number with the
+baseline the course keeps in `course/tracks/jobq/budget/3.25-release-artifacts.toml`. They are
+counts rather than seconds, so the gate answers the same thing on a loaded machine as on an idle
+one, and the two upper bounds are the promise this page is about: nothing in the release went
+unchecked, and nothing got in that the release does not declare.
 
